{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Of David. A Maskil. Happy is he whose transgression is lifted</b> — It is possible that this psalm follows the pattern of a piyyut, its beginning being 'Maskil,' or because 'I will instruct you' is written in it." "<b>And the word 'whose transgression is lifted'</b> — The masters of the alef [i.e., those who parse it with an alef] follow the pattern of the masters of the heh [i.e., those who parse nasa with a heh], or both are found. And the proof: 'they shall bear their disgrace' (כי וכסה קלון is a kal participle). [The reading וכסוי is possibly corrupt; כי וכסה קלון parses the form.] And the meaning: for one who is smitten or afflicted on account of a transgression he committed openly — therefore he said, 'I will declare my transgression against myself.' And there is a difficult question: behold we find David saying, 'For I am a devout man' and 'You will not give over Your devout one'; how then do we find in several psalms that he has transgressions and iniquities, as 'My transgression I know'? And the answer: for his heart was whole with HaShem, and he did not transgress or sin — only toward other people he has unintentional transgressions. And others said: that he speaks on behalf of others who are in exile. If so, what does one do with 'My transgression I know,' which concerns Bathsheba?! And I shall explain it further."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:29:15.780788155Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}